# Vector

{{ template "chart.versionBadge" . }}{{ template "chart.typeBadge" . }}{{ template "chart.appVersionBadge" . }}

[Vector](https://vector.dev/) is a high-performance, end-to-end observability data pipeline that puts you in control of your observability data. Collect, transform, and route all your logs, metrics, and traces to any vendors you want today and any other vendors you may want tomorrow. Vector enables dramatic cost reduction, novel data enrichment, and data security where you need it, not where is most convenient for your vendors.

## How to use Vector Helm repository

You need to add this repository to your Helm repositories:

```
helm repo add vector https://helm.vector.dev
helm repo update
```

{{ template "chart.requirementsSection" . }}

## Quick start

By default, Vector runs as a `StatefulSet` in the "Aggregator" role. It can alternatively run as a `Deployment` for the "Stateless-Aggregator" role or a `DaemonSet` for the "Agent" role.

### Installing the Vector chart

To install the chart with the release name `<RELEASE_NAME>` run:

```bash
helm install <RELEASE_NAME> vector/vector
```

### Upgrading

#### Role validation

The chart accepts `Agent`, `Aggregator`, and `Stateless-Aggregator` in any casing. Empty, unknown, or non-string `role` values fail during rendering. Earlier versions accepted invalid strings without creating a Vector workload; replace those values with a supported role before upgrading.

Keep the original role casing for existing releases, because it is part of the immutable workload selector.

#### From original charts

* [Migrate from the `vector-agent` chart](docs/Migrate_from_vector-agent.md)
* [Migrate from the `vector-aggregator` chart](docs/Migrate_from_vector-aggregator.md)

### Uninstalling the chart

To uninstall/delete the `<RELEASE_NAME>` deployment:

```bash
helm delete <RELEASE_NAME>
```

The command removes all the Kubernetes components associated with the chart and deletes the release.

## Configuration

1. Using our [`default-values.yaml`](values.yaml) configuration file as a reference, create your own `values.yaml`.
2. Upgrade the Vector chart with your new `values.yaml` file:

```bash
helm upgrade -f values.yaml <RELEASE_NAME> vector/vector
```

**Vector recommends that your `values.yaml` only contain values that need to be overridden, as it allows a smoother experience when upgrading chart versions.**

See the [configuration options](#all-configuration-options) section to discover all possibilities offered by the Vector chart.

### Running on control plane nodes

Depending on your Kubernetes distribution, you may need to configure `tolerations` to run Vector on nodes acting as the control plane.

For example to run Vector on [Openshift](https://www.redhat.com/en/technologies/cloud-computing/openshift) control plane nodes you can set:

```yaml
tolerations:
  - effect: NoSchedule
    key: node-role.kubernetes.io/master
    operator: Exists
  - effect: NoSchedule
    key: node-role.kubernetes.io/infra
    operator: Exists
```

Or for [RKE](https://rancher.com/products/rke) control plane nodes set:

```yaml
tolerations:
  - effect: NoSchedule
    key: node-role.kubernetes.io/controlplane
    operator: Exists
  - effect: NoExecute
    key: node-role.kubernetes.io/etcd
    operator: Exists
```

### Using template syntax in `customConfig`

As Vector's [template syntax](https://vector.dev/docs/reference/configuration/template-syntax/) shares the same syntax as Helm templates, explicit handling is required
if you are using Vector's template syntax in the `customConfig` option. To avoid Helm templating configuration intended for Vector you can supply configuration like so:

```yaml
customConfig:
  #...
  sinks:
    loki:
      #...
      labels:
        foo: bar
        host: |-
          {{ print "{{ print \"{{ host }}\" }}" }}
        source: |-
          {{ print "{{ print \"{{ source_type }}\" }}" }}
```

### Loading configuration dynamically by label

Vector's configuration can be loaded from ConfigMaps selected by label instead of a fixed list of names (`existingConfigMaps`).

There are two options:

* `emptyConfig: true` mounts an empty `emptyDir` at `/etc/vector/` and adds `--allow-empty-config` and `--config-dir /etc/vector/` to Vector's `args`.
  Use it if you want to fill the directory yourself, for example with your own sidecar via `extraContainers` and `extraVolumeMounts`.
* `configSidecar.enabled: true` additionally runs a [k8s-sidecar](https://github.com/kiwigrid/k8s-sidecar) container that writes all ConfigMaps
  with the label `configSidecar.label` (default `vector-config`) and the value `configSidecar.labelValue` (default: the release name) to `/etc/vector/`.
  The chart also adds `--watch-config`, so Vector reloads its configuration when the files change, and creates a `Role` and `RoleBinding`
  that allow the sidecar to read ConfigMaps in the release namespace (disable with `configSidecar.rbac.create: false`).
  An init container (`config-sidecar-init`, same image and settings) loads the matching ConfigMaps once before Vector starts,
  because settings such as the API address cannot be changed by a reload.

With only `configSidecar.enabled: true`, the sidecar also loads the ConfigMap generated by the chart, which already defines
the sinks `prom_exporter` and `stdout`. Component IDs must be unique across all loaded files, so use other names in your own ConfigMaps:

```yaml
configSidecar:
  enabled: true
```

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: my-vector-config
  labels:
    vector-config: <RELEASE_NAME>
data:
  my-sinks.yaml: |
    sinks:
      extra_metrics_console:
        type: console
        inputs: [internal_metrics]
        encoding:
          codec: json
```

Things to be aware of:

* Only files ending in `.yaml`, `.yml`, `.json` or `.toml` are picked up by Vector.
* With `configSidecar.enabled`, the sidecar mounts a service account token and only ConfigMaps in the release namespace are loaded.
* The sidecar writes to `/etc/vector/`, so `--config-dir` in `args` must point to the same directory. Other values fail rendering.
* `customConfig.data_dir` must not be set, because Vector starts with its default `/var/lib/vector` before the sidecar delivers a configuration.
* `existingConfigMaps` is not mounted when `configSidecar` is enabled. Label the ConfigMaps instead.
* The init container needs the same RBAC as the sidecar. ConfigMaps that are created after the Pod started are only picked up by the sidecar,
  so settings that Vector cannot change at runtime (for example `api.address`) have to be present in a labeled ConfigMap before the Pod starts.
* If `emptyConfig` or `existingConfigMaps` is set, the chart does not derive ports from its default configuration.
  Set `containerPorts` and `service.ports` to match the configuration you load.
  With `emptyConfig` the (non-headless) Service is only created if `service.ports` is set, because Kubernetes requires ports for it.
  The `podMonitor` scrapes the container port named by `podMonitor.port` (default `prom-exporter`), so define a port with this name in `containerPorts`
  or adjust `podMonitor.port`. Ingress backends reference the ports you configure in `ingress.hosts` and `service.ports`.
* Vector's `data_dir` is not set by the chart when `configSidecar` or `emptyConfig` is used, so `/var/lib/vector/` (the `data` volume) is used.
  If a loaded configuration sets a different `data_dir`, its state is not stored on that volume.
* Vector keeps running with the previous configuration if a reloaded configuration is invalid. Check Vector's logs after changing a ConfigMap.
* Other [k8s-sidecar settings](https://github.com/kiwigrid/k8s-sidecar#configuration-environment-variables) can be set with `configSidecar.extraEnv`.
  The chart only creates a namespaced `Role`, so loading ConfigMaps from other namespaces requires your own RBAC.

### API exposure and health probes

When using chart-managed default configuration (without `customConfig`, `existingConfigMaps` and `emptyConfig`),
Vector's API listens on `0.0.0.0:8686`, and the chart applies a default readiness probe
(`grpc` on port `8686`).

If you use `customConfig`, ensure `api.enabled`/`api.address` and probe settings are aligned
with your configuration.

As this API can expose internal state, restrict reachability with Kubernetes controls such as
`NetworkPolicy` and carefully scoped Service/Ingress exposure.

## All configuration options

The following table lists the configurable parameters of the Vector chart and their default values. Specify each parameter using the `--set key=value[,key=value]` argument to `helm install`. For example,

```bash
helm install <RELEASE_NAME> \
  vector/vector \
  --set role=Agent
```

